      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. one more thing—when we're looking at all these gates, what's the biggest risk we're actually trying to defend against? like, what's the worst thing that happens if a gate fails or if something slips through the cracks?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When looking at these gates, the biggest risk you're trying to defend against is the risk of **production downtime**, data loss, or partial data loss due to deployed code that has not been thoroughly tested or validated. This can happen if:\n\n* Deployed code contains bugs that crash your application or lead to data corruption\n* Untested or unstable dependencies cause the application to fail\n* Code changes introduce unintended side effects or cascading dependencies that break existing functionality\n* Regressions from testing aren't caught and are deployed to production, which can affect other features or services\n\nBy having gates in place, you're ensuring that code that does not meet certain quality standards (i.e., build integrity, code health, test coverage) is blocked from reaching production, and thus reduces the risk of production downtime or data loss.",
      "source": "target",
